# Which mating patterns should you know by sight?
voices: {"a":"gemini:Charon | calm, warm chess coach","b":"gemini:Puck | eager club player, relaxed and good-humoured; never exaggerated"}
prompt: "What do back-rank, smothered, Anastasia's and Arabian mates have in common? Why does the Greek gift sacrifice on h7 work, and when does it fail?"
next: {"title":"Why do in-between moves wreck your calculation?","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/chess-30-days/12-why-do-in-between-moves-wreck-your.cast"}

## Warm-up: skewer, then the guard
use: chess_board
with:
    positions:
      - {fen: 3r4/5ppp/8/3k4/8/8/5PPP/R5K1 w - - 0 1, moves: [Rd1+, Ke6, Rxd8]}
      - {fen: 2b2rk1/ppp1qppp/5n2/6B1/7Q/3B4/PPP2PPP/R5K1 w - - 0 1, moves: [Bxf6, h6, Bxe7]}
    position_shown: 0
    plies_shown: 0

    card title Warm-up

A: Today: which mates you should know by sight. First, two warm-up puzzles from last week.
    draw position

B: Black's king is out in the open. Rook to a5, check!
    move piece_a1 to.ref sq_a5 duration 1

A: A check, but the king just steps aside, and you've won nothing.
    move piece_a1 to.ref sq_a1 duration 1

    pause 0.3

    ask "Win material." widget chess line true hints ["A check on the d-file.","What stands behind the king?"] themes ["skewer"] voice a
        right: Rook to d1, check: the king steps to e6, and the rook takes the rook on d8.
        wrong: Look for a check along the line that runs through the king and the rook.

B: The king had to move, and the rook behind it fell.
    highlight piece_d8

A: Second one. White's queen and bishop both aim at h7.
    animate position_shown 1 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

B: Then queen takes h7, check!
    highlight piece_h4 piece_d3

A: And the knight on f6 takes your queen. It guards h7.
    highlight piece_f6

    pause 0.3

    ask "Win material." widget chess line true hints ["Which black piece guards h7?","Take the guard."] themes ["removing the defender"] voice a
        right: Bishop takes f6, hitting the queen and the guard that matters. Taking back allows mate on h7, so Black plays pawn to h6, and the bishop takes the queen.
        wrong: Before you take on h7, take the piece that guards it.

B: So Black had to choose: the king or the queen.
    highlight piece_e7

A: That knight was guarding a square next to the king. Today: what happens when the king's own neighbours shut it in.
    highlight piece_g8

## Four mates, one secret
use: chess_board
with:
    positions:
      - {fen: 6k1/ppq2ppp/8/8/8/3Q4/PPr2PPP/4R1K1 w - - 0 1, moves: [Re8#]}
      - {fen: q5rk/pp4pp/8/6N1/8/8/PP2QPPP/6K1 w - - 0 1, moves: [Nf7#]}
      - {fen: 1q3r1k/pp2Npp1/8/8/8/3R4/PP3PPP/6K1 w - - 0 1, moves: [Rh3#]}
      - {fen: r6k/3R3p/5Np1/8/8/1q6/5PPP/6K1 w - - 0 1, moves: [Rxh7#]}
      - {fen: rnbq1rk1/pppn1ppp/4p3/3pP3/1b1P4/2NB1N2/PPP2PPP/R1BQ1RK1 w - - 0 1, moves: [Bxh7+, Kxh7, Ng5+, Kg8, Qh5]}
      - {fen: rnbq1rk1/pppnbppp/4p3/3pP3/3P4/2NB1N2/PPP2PPP/R1BQ1RK1 w - - 0 1, moves: [Bxh7+, Kxh7, Ng5+, Bxg5]}
    position_shown: 0
    plies_shown: 0
sources:
    - id: heinse
      title: Anastasia und das Schachspiel
      authors: Wilhelm Heinse
      year: 1803
      finding: The novel that gave Anastasia's mate its name.
      url: https://www.chesshistory.com/winter/extra/anastasia.html
    - id: greekgift
      title: Greek gift sacrifice
      authors: Wikipedia
      year: 2026
      finding: The name's origin is not clear; the usual explanation is the Trojan horse. The conditions for the sacrifice, and the line 1.e4 e6 2.d4 d5 3.Nc3 Nf6 4.e5 Nfd7 5.Nf3 Bb4 6.Bd3 O-O 7.Bxh7+ Kxh7 8.Ng5+ Kg8 9.Qh5.
      url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Greek_gift_sacrifice

    card title "Four mates, one secret"

A: Now four mates every club player should see at a glance. They look different, but they share one secret.
    draw position

B: Black's rook is on my second rank. I'd defend b2.
    highlight piece_c2

A: Checks first. Black's king sits behind three pawns that never moved.
    highlight piece_f7 piece_g7 piece_h7

    pause 0.3

    ask "Mate in one." widget chess line true hints ["Which check can the king not escape?","The e-file is open."] themes ["back rank","mating pattern"] voice a
        right: Rook to e8: the king's own pawns hold it in, and nothing can block or take.
        wrong: Look at the king's free squares. Is there a check on the back rank?

B: One rook did it. The pawns did the rest.
    highlight piece_e8

A: That's the back-rank mate. Now a king boxed in on every side.
    animate position_shown 1 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

    pause 0.3

    ask "Mate in one." widget chess line true hints ["Only one piece can jump the wall.","The knight on g5."] themes ["smothered mate","mating pattern"] voice a
        right: Knight to f7, mate. Its own rook and pawns box the king in: the smothered mate.
        wrong: The king has no free square at all. Which piece can check it over the wall?

B: It can't move, and nothing can take the knight.
    highlight piece_f7

A: Next, a knight and a rook together.
    animate position_shown 2 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

A: The knight on e7 guards g8, and the pawn on g7 blocks its own king.
    highlight piece_e7 piece_g7

A: Rook to h3, mate. It's called Anastasia's mate, after a German novel from 1803.
    animate plies_shown 1 duration 1.2

A: And the Arabian mate.
    animate position_shown 3 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

A: The knight guards the rook, and takes away g8.
    animate plies_shown 1 duration 1.2

    highlight piece_f6 duration 1.5

B: So what do all four have in common?
    highlight piece_h8

A: The king is stuck: its own men, the board's edge or the attackers take its squares. The attacker only has to check, and cover what's left.
    highlight sq_g8 sq_g7 effect circle

A: So count the enemy king's free squares. One, or none, and look for the check.
    highlight piece_h7

A: Now a sacrifice that cracks that wall open: the Greek gift.
    animate position_shown 4 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

B: Bishop takes h7, check! I always want to play that.
    highlight piece_d3

A: Bishop takes, check,
    animate plies_shown 1 duration 1.2

A: and the king takes.
    animate plies_shown 2 duration 1.2

A: Knight to g5, check,
    animate plies_shown 3 duration 1.2

A: and the king goes back.
    animate plies_shown 4 duration 1.2

B: What if the king comes out to g6 instead?
    highlight piece_g8

A: Worse. Out in the open it walks into checks, starting with queen to d3.
    highlight piece_d1

A: Queen to h5.
    animate plies_shown 5 duration 1.2

A: Mate on h7 is coming, and only giving up the queen avoids being mated.
    highlight sq_h7 effect circle

    pause 0.5

A: Why a Greek gift? Nobody is quite sure. The usual guess is the Trojan horse: a gift with an army inside.
    highlight piece_g5

A: Now put Black's bishop on e7. Does bishop takes h7 still work?
    animate position_shown 5 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

    quiz "Black's bishop now stands on e7. Does Bxh7+ still work?" on_canvas true buttons_at.x 905 buttons_at.y 400 buttons_layout column say_question false voice a
        right: No. After bishop takes h7, king takes, and knight to g5, check, the bishop on e7 takes the knight.
        wrong: After knight to g5, check, look at which black piece reaches g5.
        * Yes, it works
        + No: Bxg5
        * Only if Kh8

A: Bishop takes, check,
    animate plies_shown 1 duration 1.2

A: the king takes,
    animate plies_shown 2 duration 1.2

A: knight to g5, check,
    animate plies_shown 3 duration 1.2

A: and the bishop on e7 takes it: two pieces gone for one pawn.
    animate plies_shown 4 duration 1.2

    highlight piece_g5 duration 1.5

A: So check the helpers before the gift:
    animate position_shown 4 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

A: a knight for g5, a queen for h5, a guard on g5, and no black bishop or knight that can take there.
    highlight piece_f3 piece_d1 piece_c1

## Your turn: five mates
feedback:
    style: dry
    perfect: Five for five. Kings everywhere should be nervous.
    good: Most of them. The patterns will start to jump out at you.
    poor: Count the king's free squares first, then try again.
    none: The kings will wait for you.
use: chess_board
with:
    positions:
      - {fen: kr6/pp6/8/3N4/7q/8/5PPP/6K1 w - - 0 1, moves: [Nc7#]}
      - {fen: 7k/pp5p/5N2/8/8/8/PPq4P/6RK w - - 0 1, moves: [Rg8#]}
      - {fen: 4r1k1/5ppp/8/4q3/8/8/3Q1PPP/4R1K1 b - - 0 1, moves: [Qxe1+, Qxe1, Rxe1#], flip: true}
      - {fen: 6r1/6p1/7p/3k4/8/1P6/5PPP/5BK1 w - - 0 1, moves: [Bc4+, Kc6, Bxg8]}
      - {fen: 1q3rk1/pp3ppp/8/3N4/7Q/5R2/PP3PPP/6K1 w - - 0 1, moves: [Ne7+, Kh8, Qxh7+, Kxh7, Rh3#]}
    position_shown: 0
    plies_shown: 0

    card title "Your turn"

A: Now you've seen the four patterns and the gift: your turn. Five puzzles, mostly mates.
    draw position

    pause 0.3

    ask "Mate in one." widget chess line true hints ["Count the king's free squares.","Only a knight can jump in."] themes ["smothered mate","mating pattern"] voice a
        right: Knight to c7, mate: the rook and the pawns wall the king in.
        wrong: The king has no free square. Which piece can check it over its own men?

B: The same smothered mate, in the other corner.
    highlight piece_c7

A: Next, a rook and a knight.
    animate position_shown 1 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

    pause 0.3

    ask "Mate in one." widget chess line true hints ["The g-file is open.","The knight on f6 guards g8."] themes ["mating pattern"] voice a
        right: Rook to g8, mate: the knight guards the rook, and the pawn on h7 blocks its own king.
        wrong: Which square next to the king does your knight already guard?

A: Now you're Black: the board turns round.
    animate position_shown 2 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

B: White's king is stuck behind its own pawns too.
    highlight piece_g1

    pause 0.3

    ask "Mate in two." widget chess line true hints ["Your queen and rook both aim at e1.","Give the queen."] themes ["back rank","mating pattern"] voice a
        right: Queen takes e1, check: the queen takes back, and your rook takes again. Mate on the back rank.
        wrong: Your queen and rook both aim at e1, and only White's queen guards it. Which one goes in first?

A: One from last week. You're White again.
    animate position_shown 3 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

    pause 0.3

    ask "Win material." widget chess line true hints ["A check on a diagonal.","What stands behind the king?"] themes ["skewer"] voice a
        right: Bishop to c4, check: the pawn on b3 guards it. The king steps to c6, and the bishop takes the rook on g8.
        wrong: Find a check on the line through the king and the rook.

A: The pawn on b3 made that check safe.
    highlight piece_b3

A: Last one: the hardest. Black's king has a wall of pawns.
    animate position_shown 4 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

    pause 0.3

    ask "Mate in three." widget chess line true hints ["Start with a knight check.","Then give the queen on h7."] themes ["mating pattern","combination"] voice a
        right: Knight to e7, check, and the king goes to h8. Queen takes h7, check, the king takes, and the rook mates on h3: Anastasia's mate.
        wrong: Which knight check guards g8 and g6? Then open the h-file.

B: I gave the queen, and the pattern did the rest.
    highlight piece_h3

A: So when the king's own men surround it, count its free squares, then look for the check that takes the last one.
    highlight piece_e7 piece_g7

A: Homework: one slow game today, fifteen plus ten or slower. Then find its first mistake.
    erase position

## Where next
end_page: true

